WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:04.470
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:08.930
As machine learning research advances over
time, learning-based techniques are getting

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:14.840
better and better at generating images, or
even creating videos when given a topic.

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:20.140
A few episodes ago, we talked about a DeepMind’s
Dual Video Discriminator technique, in which,

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:25.390
multiple neural networks compete against each
other, teaching our machines to synthesize

00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:28.520
a collection of 2-second long videos.

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:33.250
One of the key advantages of this method was
that it also learned the concept of changes

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:39.290
in the camera view, zooming in on an object,
and understood that if someone draws something

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:43.170
with a pen, the ink has to remain on the paper
unchanged.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:48.720
However, generally, if we wish to ask an AI
to synthesize assets for us, in many cases,

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:52.370
we’ll likely have an exact idea of what
we are looking for.

00:00:52.370 --> 00:00:56.530
In these cases, we are looking for a little
more artistic control than this technique

00:00:56.530 --> 00:00:57.810
offers us.

00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:00.090
So, can we get around this?

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:01.840
If so, how?

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:03.000
Well, we can!

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:08.159
I’ll tell you how in a moment, but to understand
this solution, we first have to have a firm

00:01:08.159 --> 00:01:11.479
grasp on the concept of latent spaces.

00:01:11.479 --> 00:01:17.140
You can think of a latent space as a compressed
representation that tries to capture the essence

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:19.530
of the dataset that we have at hand.

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:22.793
You can see a similar latent space method
in action here that captures the key features

00:01:22.793 --> 00:01:29.039
that set different kinds of fonts apart and
presents these options on a 2D plane, and

00:01:29.039 --> 00:01:34.000
here, you see our technique that builds a
latent space for modeling a wide range of

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.749
photorealistic material models that we can
explore.

00:01:38.749 --> 00:01:41.039
And now to this new work.

00:01:41.039 --> 00:01:46.229
What this tries to do is find a path in the
latent space of these images that relates

00:01:46.229 --> 00:01:51.130
to intuitive concepts like camera zooming,
rotation or shifting.

00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:56.100
That’s not an easy task, but if we can pull
it off, we’ll have more artistic control

00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:01.130
over these generated images, which would be
immensely useful for many creative tasks.

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:07.459
This new work can perform that, and not only
that, but it is also able to learn the concept

00:02:07.459 --> 00:02:13.880
of color enhancement, and can even increase
or decrease the contrast of these images.

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:19.629
The key idea of this paper is that this can
be done through trying to find crazy, non-linear

00:02:19.629 --> 00:02:25.510
trajectories in these latent spaces that happen
to relate to these intuitive concepts.

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:31.120
It is not perfect in a sense that we can indeed
zoom in on the picture of this dog, but the

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:36.939
posture of the dog also changes, and it even
seems like we’re starting out with a puppy

00:02:36.939 --> 00:02:39.170
that grows up frame by frame.

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:43.640
This means that we have learned to navigate
this latent space, but there is still some

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:48.360
additional fat in these movements, which is
a typical side effect of latent space-based

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:54.000
techniques and also, don’t forget that the
training data the AI is given also has its

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:55.200
own limits.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:01.439
However, as you see, we are now one step closer
to not only having an AI that synthesizes

00:03:01.439 --> 00:03:08.680
images for us, but one that does it exactly
with the camera setup, rotation, and colors

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:10.340
that we are looking for.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:11.900
What a time to be alive!

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:17.760
If you wish to see beautiful formulations
of walks…walks in latent spaces, that is,

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:20.580
make sure to have a look at the paper in the
video description.

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:27.209
Also, note that we have now appeared on instagram
with bite-sized pieces of our bite-sized videos.

00:03:27.209 --> 00:03:29.680
Yes, it is quite peculiar.

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:34.299
Make sure to check it out, just search for
two minute papers on instagram or click the

00:03:34.299 --> 00:03:35.950
link in the video description.

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:39.189
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

